{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS8xSZvfHMT5CjNDt8F2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed three separate bugs, all needed before Connect works. I tested the full connect flow against a local fake Intervals server, not the real intervals.icu.\n\n1. **Connect returned \"unauthorized\" for everyone.** The button was a plain link, so the browser opened a new page. A new page doesn't carry the sign-in token, which the app only adds to its own background requests. Connect now asks the server for the Intervals link through a signed-in request, then sends the browser there (`server.py`, `static/app.js`).\n2. **Intervals refused the request before showing the consent screen.** Since calendar sync shipped on Oct 2, the requested permissions name Activity and Calendar twice each (read and write). Intervals rejects that, because write already includes read. It is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` (`config.py`), which grants the same access. Intervals' error only says `invalid_request` and doesn't name the problem. The timing and your colleague's note point here, but Intervals itself hasn't confirmed it.\n3. **Rivera was told to reconnect after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire and have no refresh token. Our server assumed a one-hour lifetime, and a background job then threw away Rivera's valid token. I removed that job and the made-up expiry. If the athlete revokes access on Intervals, the next 401 from Intervals now clears the token and the card shows \"revoked, reconnect\".\n\nIn the test, signing in and connecting worked, wellness data came back, and after a simulated revoke the app switched to \"reconnect\" without crashing. The old permission list was rejected by the fake server, but only because I built it to reject that.\n\n- **Rivera has to connect once more.** Their old token was already thrown away; the button now works for that.\n- **Other servers on this machine:** something else was already running on ports 8766 and 8777. I left both alone and tested on 18766/18799.\n\nFrom your colleague's note, three parts applied: the init endpoint must be a signed-in POST, don't list the same area twice, and tokens never expire so handle revocation on a 401. The \"send only three fields when exchanging the code\" advice didn't apply: our extra fields already worked for Rivera, so I left them. I didn't switch `state` to a JWT; our signed `state` already carries the user and expires after 15 minutes. I also didn't add the reconnect account check or encrypt stored tokens. Neither caused these failures, but production should encrypt tokens since they never expire.\n\nANSWER.md has the full write-up and the evidence for each cause. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2235,"cache_read_input_tokens":41133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2235},"output_tokens":658,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec1fb7ea-0e15-4e12-afc7-1a3e8c0a510f","uuid":"9e2703f4-69f6-41fd-8610-4b280697b6be","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:49:12.977Z","request_id":"req_011CgS8xSJo3vw93sNJQk9vv"}
